{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive 'critical preference' grounded in the logic of verisimilitude and the aim of truth-likeness, not probability.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude cannot be formally defined without collapsing into probability (as Miller/Tichý argued).","If the choice of which theory to test next inherently assumes the future will resemble the past."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between 'probability' (inductive support) and 'corroboration' (report of past test performance). The preference is justified because the better-corroborated theory has withstood more severe attempts at falsification, making it the best candidate for having higher verisimilitude (closeness to truth), even if its probability remains zero or undefined. Acting on it is a rational methodological rule, not an inductive inference about the future.","optional_refs":["82d932993cea062a6ca0f4ca09c967de8d45396d0d458593d653fb6cb41f9376","48c4058b65566c1916052587394c85e201b43ffc6160053f0587893fb9cd43a7"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles in induction, rendering Popper's position internally inconsistent when moving from logic to action.","counterconditions":["If a non-inductive bridge (like verisimilitude) can successfully link past performance to future choice.","If 'preference' can be redefined as a purely arbitrary or aesthetic choice unrelated to expected outcomes."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Any decision to prefer Theory A over Theory B for future action logically presupposes that the properties which made A successful in the past (surviving tests) are relevant to its success in the future. This relevance assumption is the principle of induction (uniformity of 