nduction, even pragmatic.","If the 'decision to stop testing' can be fully derived from the logic of the situation without convention."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Argues that while corroboration reports are non-inductive, the *transition* to action involves a conventionalist decision to treat the current best theory as 'true enough' for practical purposes. This admits a 'pragmatic induction' (we act as if the future resembles the past) which Popper implicitly accepted but refused to label as such to protect his anti-inductivist stance. The surviving view is that the preference is defensible pragmatically but indefensible logically.","optional_refs":["5d848ac46e0509ec2b1adfe65e52e96fdfe97e2315ce500bba4be1bfb57964df","469214066cd0fe367482ab1dbf622f3c2ad7e94d48942d7e905fb1fd4b3ff862"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}